{"name":"Earthenware","hex":"#a89373","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"earthenware","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An opaque, semi-porous ceramic made from clay and other compounds."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/earthenware"}],"entries":[{"title":"Earthenware","blocks":[{"text":"Earthenware is glazed or unglazed nonvitreous pottery that has normally been fired below 1,200 °C (2,190 °F). Basic earthenware, often called terracotta, absorbs liquids such as water. However, earthenware can be made impervious to liquids by coating it with a ceramic glaze, and such a process is used for the great majority of modern domestic earthenware. The main other important types of pottery are porcelain, bone china, and stoneware, all fired at high enough temperatures to vitrify. End applications include tableware and decorative ware such as figurines."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Samanid_plate._Attributed_to_Nishapur%2C_Iran%2C_10th_century_%28Metropolitan_Museum_of_Art%2C_67.178.2%29.jpg/330px-Samanid_plate._Attributed_to_Nishapur%2C_Iran%2C_10th_century_%28Metropolitan_Museum_of_Art%2C_67.178.2%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earthenware"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Top_Section_of_a_Water_Jug%2C_late_12th-early_13th_century.jpg/500px-Top_Section_of_a_Water_Jug%2C_late_12th-early_13th_century.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Top_Section_of_a_Water_Jug%2C_late_12th-early_13th_century.jpg/500px-Top_Section_of_a_Water_Jug%2C_late_12th-early_13th_century.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATop_Section_of_a_Water_Jug%2C_late_12th-early_13th_century.jpg"},{"srcse